22.4  Exit codes

TL;DR
  - Every command ends with a number: 0 means success, anything else (1 to 255)
    a failure.
  - $? holds the last one. && runs the next command only on success, || only on
    failure.
  - 126: found but not executable. 127: command not found. 128 + N: killed by
    signal N, so 130 is Ctrl-C and 137 is SIGKILL.

      grep -q error app.log; echo $?
      make && ./install.sh
      cd build || exit 1

Pitfalls
  - In a pipeline, $? is the code of the last command: false | true succeeds.
    set -o pipefail makes any failure count.
  - grep returns 1 when nothing matches: not an error, but a script with set -e
    stops there. Write grep … || true when no match is fine.
  - set -e doesn't stop on a failure inside an if condition or on the left of &&
    and ||.
  - Codes wrap around at 256: exit 256 gives 0.

Deep dive

  ▾ Where does the number come from?

    The program passes it to the kernel when it exits, and the parent reads it
    with wait(), the same call that clears zombies (see 12.1). The shell adds
    the conventions for 126, 127 and 128 + N. Some programs give meaning to
    their codes: for diff and grep, 1 means "different" or "no match", and 2
    means an error.

  ▾ How do I write safer scripts?

    Start bash scripts with set -euo pipefail: stop at the first failure, treat
    unset variables as errors, and count failures inside pipes. Then allow the
    failures you expect, explicitly, with || true or an if.

